---
import PNotes from "../components/PNotes.astro";
---

<p-slide class="center" style="--scale-from: 3.5;--fragment-duration: 1s">
  <h3
    p-effect="zoom fade"
    style="font-size: 0.6em; opacity: 0.6; margin-block-end: 0.5em;"
  >
    your MCP server is on the internet now...
  </h3>
  <h2 p-effect="zoom fade" style="font-size: 1.5em;">
    <span class="main-gradient">who's <span class="accent">allowed</span> to call it?</span> 🤔
  </h2>
  <p
    p-effect="zoom fade"
    style="font-size: 0.6em; margin-block-start: 1em; opacity: 0.7;"
  >
    MCP answers this with <strong>OAuth 2.1</strong>
  </p>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      stdio is inherently more controlled: the host starts a local process and
      communicates with it directly. But a remote HTTP MCP server is exposed
      over the network, so we need a standard way to authorize clients and
      users.
    </p>
    <p>
      MCP does not invent a custom auth mechanism here. It builds on OAuth 2.1
      and standard OAuth metadata discovery.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide class="center">
  <h2 style="margin-block-end: 0.2rem;" class="cover-internal-sub-section-all">
    HTTP <span class="accent">Authentication</span>
  </h2>
  <h3 style="margin-block-start: 0.8rem;">OAuth 2.1 🔐</h3>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      For remote MCP servers, authorization is handled at the HTTP transport
      layer. The MCP server behaves like a protected resource server. The OAuth
      authorization server can be the same application, or an external identity
      provider.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3 class="cover-internal-sub-section-all">The <span class="accent">OAuth flow</span> in MCP</h3>
  <ul style="font-size: 0.8em">
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      🚪 <span class="main-sub-gradient">1. Unauthorized request</span> — client
      calls the MCP endpoint without a token
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🔍 <span class="main-sub-gradient">2. Resource discovery</span> — server
      points to its protected resource metadata
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      🧭 <span class="main-sub-gradient">3. Auth server discovery</span> —
      client finds authorization and token endpoints
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🪪 <span class="main-sub-gradient">4. Client registration</span> — client
      ID as a metadata URL (CIMD), DCR as fallback
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      👤 <span class="main-sub-gradient">5. Authorization</span> — user logs in
      and grants permission
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🎫 <span class="main-sub-gradient">6. Token exchange</span> — client gets
      an access token using PKCE
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      ✅ <span class="main-sub-gradient">7. Authenticated requests</span> —
      token sent as <code>Authorization: Bearer</code>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      The important MCP-specific part is the discovery chain. The client does
      not start by guessing the authorization endpoint. First it discovers the
      protected resource metadata for the MCP server.
    </p>
    <p>
      That metadata tells the client which authorization server should be used.
      Then the client reads the authorization server metadata to find the login
      endpoint, token endpoint, supported PKCE methods, and so on.
    </p>
    <p>
      Client registration changed in the latest specs. The preferred way is now
      Client ID Metadata Documents: the client_id is an HTTPS URL pointing to a
      JSON document describing the client, so there's no registration call at
      all. Dynamic Client Registration is deprecated in 2026-07-28 and kept only
      as a fallback for authorization servers that don't support CIMD.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two hardening details: the client sends the resource parameter (RFC 8707)
      so the token is bound to this MCP server, and it validates the iss
      parameter in the authorization response (RFC 9207).
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3 style="margin-block-end: 1em;">
    <span class="cover-internal-sub-section-all">
      1. <span class="accent">Protected Resource</span> Metadata
    </span>
    🔍
  </h3>

  <pre
    class="small-line"
    data-label-file="/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp"
  >
  <code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-json">{
      "resource": "https://my-mcp-server.com/mcp",
      "authorization_servers": [
        "https://auth.my-mcp-server.com"
      ],
      "scopes_supported": [
        "orders:read",
        "orders:write"
      ],
      "bearer_methods_supported": [
        "header"
      ]
    }</code>
  </pre>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      This is the MCP-specific discovery step. The MCP server is the protected
      resource. It exposes metadata that tells the client which authorization
      server can issue valid tokens for this resource.
    </p>
    <p>
      The key field here is authorization_servers. It points to the OAuth
      authorization server. This can be the same origin as the MCP server, or a
      separate identity provider.
    </p>
    <p>
      Note the path: for a server at /mcp the document lives at
      /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp (RFC 9728 path insertion).
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3 style="margin-block-end: 1em;">
    <span class="cover-internal-sub-section-all">
      2. <span class="accent">Authorization Server</span> Metadata
    </span>
    🧭
  </h3>

  <pre
    class="small-line"
    data-label-file="https://auth.my-mcp-server.com/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server"
  >
<code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-json">
  {
    "issuer": "https://auth.my-mcp-server.com",
    "authorization_endpoint": "https://auth.my-mcp-server.com/oauth/authorize",
    "token_endpoint": "https://auth.my-mcp-server.com/oauth/token",
    "registration_endpoint": "https://auth.my-mcp-server.com/oauth/register",
    "response_types_supported": ["code"],
    "code_challenge_methods_supported": ["S256"]
  }</code>
  </pre>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      This is standard OAuth authorization server metadata. Once the MCP client
      knows which authorization server to use, it fetches this document to learn
      where to redirect the user, where to exchange the authorization code, and
      whether dynamic client registration is supported.
    </p>
    <p>
      S256 is the PKCE method we care about. The client creates a code verifier,
      sends the code challenge during authorization, and later proves possession
      of the verifier during the token exchange.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3 class="cover-internal-sub-section-all">Who does what</h3>
  <ul>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      🏛️ <span class="main-sub-gradient">Authorization server</span> — login,
      consent, issues tokens
      <span class="item-sub">
        your identity provider: <code>Keycloak</code> / <code>Auth0</code> /
        <code>Clerk</code>…
      </span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🛡️ <span class="main-sub-gradient">MCP server = resource server</span> —
      verifies tokens, never issues them
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      🧰 <span class="main-sub-gradient">MCP SDK v2</span> — ships bearer auth
      &amp; metadata helpers
    </li>
  </ul>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      This is the official position of the TypeScript SDK v2: your MCP server is
      an OAuth resource server. It verifies access tokens that an authorization
      server issued, and it never issues them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Implementing OAuth from scratch is easy to get wrong. For login, consent
      and token issuance use a dedicated identity provider. The v1
      authorization-server helpers still exist, but they are frozen in the
      server-legacy package.
    </p>
    <p>
      The MCP-specific part is not "invent OAuth again". It is wiring standard
      OAuth discovery, protected resource metadata, token validation, and the
      MCP transport together, and that is exactly what the SDK covers.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide follow-fragments>
  <h3 style="margin-block-end: 1em;">
    <span class="cover-internal-sub-section-all"><span class="accent">Protecting</span> the endpoint</span>
    🔒
  </h3>

  <pre class="small-line" data-label-file="server.js">
<code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">  import { requireBearerAuth, mcpAuthMetadataRouter }
    from "@modelcontextprotocol/express";

  </code><code p-fragment p-effect="highlight" style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">app.use(mcpAuthMetadataRouter({ oauthMetadata, resourceServerUrl }));</code><code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">

  app.all("/mcp",
    </code><code p-fragment p-effect="highlight" style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">requireBearerAuth({ verifier, resourceMetadataUrl }),</code><code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">
    mcpHandler
  );</code>
  </pre>

  <p p-fragment p-effect="zoom" class="verifier-caption">
    <code>verifier</code> = one function:
    <code>token → &#123; clientId, scopes, expiresAt &#125;</code>
    <span class="item-sub">JWKS, introspection, or ask your IdP</span>
  </p>

  <PNotes>
    <p>This is all the code: two SDK helpers.</p>
    <p>
      The router publishes the "sign on the door": which server gives you the
      token.
    </p>
    <p>
      requireBearerAuth is the bouncer: no valid token, 401 pointing to the
      sign. Wrong permissions, 403.
    </p>
    <p>
      The verifier is the only part you write: a function that takes a token and
      returns who the client is, what it can do and when it expires. How is up
      to you: JWKS, introspection, or a call to your identity provider.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<style lang="scss">
  ul {
    li[p-fragment]:not(:last-of-type) {
      margin-block-end: 0.75em;
    }
  }

  .item-sub {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.72em;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--ink-mute, #484848);
    margin-inline-start: 1.5em;
    margin-block-start: 0.2em;

    code {
      font-style: normal;
      font-size: 0.95em;
    }
  }

  .verifier-caption {
    font-size: 0.6em;
    margin-block-start: 1em;
    text-align: center;
  }
</style>
